AWS-004 — Atlas Workflow Language (AWL)

Document ID: AWS-004
Title: Atlas Workflow Language (AWL)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Atlas Workflow Language (AWL), the canonical declarative language for expressing engineering workflows within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

AWL provides a standardized, implementation-independent method for defining workflow semantics, activities, transitions, orchestration, participants, timing constraints, synchronization, and execution behavior.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow language architecture
Activity expressions
State expressions
Transition expressions
Participant expressions
Event expressions
Orchestration semantics
Extensibility

AWL applies to every Canonical Workflow Object.

3. Canonical Definition

The Atlas Workflow Language (AWL) is a declarative language for expressing workflow semantics independently of workflow engines, orchestration platforms, or programming languages.

AWL describes execution intent.

It does not prescribe implementation.

4. Objectives

AWL SHALL:

Express workflow semantics.
Remain deterministic.
Be implementation-independent.
Support human readability.
Support machine interpretation.
Enable interoperability.
Preserve explainability.
Support extensibility.
5. Language Principles

Every AWL definition SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Declarative

AWL SHALL describe what work is performed.

It SHALL NOT prescribe how execution engines implement it.

Semantic

AWL SHALL preserve engineering meaning independently of execution technology.

Deterministic

Equivalent workflow definitions SHALL produce equivalent execution semantics.

Readable

Workflow definitions SHALL remain understandable by engineers.

Composable

Workflow fragments SHALL support modular composition.

Versioned

Every AWL definition SHALL reference a workflow version.

6. Canonical Language Structure

Every AWL workflow SHALL define:

Element	Description
Workflow Identity	Workflow identifier
Activities	Units of work
States	Workflow states
Transitions	State changes
Participants	Execution actors
Events	Execution events
Inputs	Required artifacts
Outputs	Produced artifacts
7. Activity Expressions

Every activity SHALL define:

Activity identifier
Activity type
Preconditions
Inputs
Outputs
Responsible participant
Expected duration
Success criteria
Failure criteria

Activities SHALL remain independently identifiable.

8. State Expressions

AWL SHALL support explicit workflow states.

Example conceptual model:

Initialized

↓

Ready

↓

Executing

↓

Waiting

↓

Completed

States SHALL remain explicit.

Implicit states SHALL NOT exist.

9. Transition Expressions

Every transition SHALL define:

Source state
Target state
Trigger
Transition condition
Generated events
Responsible authority

Transitions SHALL be deterministic.

10. Event Expressions

AWL SHALL support canonical workflow events.

Examples include:

Workflow Started
Activity Started
Activity Completed
Activity Failed
Approval Requested
Approval Granted
Validation Passed
Workflow Completed

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

11. Participant Expressions

Participants MAY include:

Engineers
Architects
Contractors
Organizations
External systems
AI agents
Digital Twins
Automated services

Each participant SHALL define:

Identity
Role
Responsibilities
Authority
Trust requirements
12. Timing Expressions

AWL SHALL support:

Deadlines
Timeouts
Delays
Scheduling constraints
Effective periods
Service-level objectives (SLOs)

Timing SHALL remain implementation-independent.

13. Branching Expressions

AWL SHALL support:

Branch Type	Description
Sequential	Ordered execution
Conditional	Context-dependent execution
Parallel	Simultaneous execution
Exclusive	Exactly one path
Inclusive	One or more paths

Branch semantics SHALL be deterministic.

14. Synchronization Expressions

AWL SHALL support synchronization including:

Join
Merge
Barrier
Wait-for-all
Wait-for-any
Completion synchronization

Synchronization SHALL preserve execution determinism.

15. Orchestration Expressions

AWL SHALL support orchestration primitives including:

Invoke workflow
Invoke activity
Wait
Retry
Escalate
Cancel
Suspend
Resume
Complete

These primitives define intent only.

Execution engines determine implementation.

16. Validation

Every AWL definition SHALL be validated for:

Structural correctness
Semantic correctness
State consistency
Transition completeness
Participant validity
Policy compatibility
Version compatibility

Validation SHALL conform to ADS-015 — Validation Model.

17. Conformance

An AWL implementation conforms if it:

Supports canonical workflow constructs.
Preserves declarative semantics.
Supports deterministic execution.
Supports workflow composition.
Preserves explainability.
Maintains interoperability.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-generated workflow definitions.
Digital Twin orchestration syntax.
Domain-specific workflow vocabularies.
Formal workflow verification.
Distributed orchestration semantics.
Executable workflow profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical AWL semantics.

